# `merge=jscpd-baseline` runs tools/scripts/merge-jscpd-baseline.mjs on the committed clone baseline. The file is a set of
# fingerprints with a pair count each, so it merges as one: per fingerprint, ours + theirs - base, kept when positive. A
# removal on either side drops it, an addition on either side keeps it. A line-based merge conflicts here far more often
# than not, because every consolidation regenerates the file and hashes sort randomly, so removals from parallel branches
# land on adjacent lines. A side that is not a baseline, or a version mismatch, stops as a conflict whose file cannot parse
# as a baseline. The result is exactly what regenerating from the merged tree gives only when the two sides change disjoint
# clone groups; a count map cannot see the merged tree, so otherwise the merge can leave a count short (the `check-clones`
# lane in `pnpm check` goes red on the merged tree: run `node .claude/rules/check-clones.mjs --update` there) or stale (the
# lane stays green and the baseline over-allows until an `--update` drops it: run one on main after a batch of merges).
#
# The driver is registered per clone by the root `prepare` script (runs on every pnpm install), unchained. A clone that has
# not run it has no `jscpd-baseline` driver defined, and git falls back to its DEFAULT three-way merge — clean on disjoint
# changes, ordinary conflict markers on overlapping ones. Never a silent keep, so an unregistered clone is degraded, not
# dangerous.
#
# `-X theirs` / `-X ours` do NOT reach this file: a strategy option is ignored wherever a custom driver owns the path. Take a
# side with `git checkout --theirs -- .jscpd-baseline.json` (or `--ours`), then regenerate.
/.jscpd-baseline.json        merge=jscpd-baseline
